{
  "requirement": "Before that commit, the `habeas-requirements` skill MUST direct agents to revert the drafts the user rejected or deferred and to list them in their report.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "structural: The only clause is: \"Before that commit, the `habeas-requirements` skill MUST direct agents to revert the drafts the user rejected or deferred and to list them in their report.\" It states what the skill's content must tell agents. It prescribes no library, algorithm, signature, type, field or delegation through a named interface. It specifies observable behavior of the document. The skill text could say this in a different step layout or with different wording, such as `git checkout` instead of `git restore -p`, and it would still satisfy the clause. So no structural obligation needs implementation evidence.\n\nThe supplied root, `assets/skills/habeas-requirements/SKILL.md`, does contain the direction. Under `section[Habeas%20requirements]/section[Workflow]`, step 5 \"Commit\" says: \"Revert drafts the user rejected or deferred (`git restore -p`) and list them in your report.\" It comes in the commit step, so it applies before the commit. The root supports the requirement.\n\nbehavioral: The requirement has two obligations. The `habeas-requirements` skill must direct agents to revert the drafts the user rejected or deferred. It must also direct them to list those drafts in their report. Both come before the commit.\n\nThe test `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_requirements_skill_reverts_rejected_and_deferred_drafts_before_committing]` covers all three points. It uses `step_of` to find the workflow step containing \"all groups together\", which is the commit step. It uses `step` to take that step's text. It then calls `assert_text_in_order`, which panics unless each phrase appears after the previous one. The phrases are:\n- \"Revert drafts the user rejected or deferred (`git restore -p`) and list them in your report\", which covers the revert directive and the report-listing directive.\n- \"then commit it\", which must come after the first phrase and so covers the \"before that commit\" ordering.\n\nIf the skill dropped the revert instruction, dropped the report instruction, or put the revert after the commit, the assertion would fail. I found no gap and no unresolved ambiguity.",
